viewer = {}

--- Changes the display mode of the viewer. Use this to render your games and simulations in fullscreen mode, fullscreen mode without buttons, or standard mode. Standard mode includes the sidebar with output and parameters, plus controls for project execution.
---@type enum
viewer.mode = nil

--- Sets the preferred framerate of the viewer. You can set this to `0`, `15`, `30`, `60` or `120`. The value `0` uses the maximum framerate of your device.
--- 
--- Note that this sets the preferred framerate. If the framerate cannot be maintained, it may drop below your preferred setting to the next lower value.
---@type number
viewer.framerate = nil

--- Setting this property to `true` indicates the renderer’s preference to lock the pointer, although the system may not honor the request. For the system to consider locking the pointer, the viewer must be running fullscreen on your device.
---@type boolean
viewer.pointerLocked = nil

--- The active runtime type, either `LEGACY` or `MODERN`.
---@type number
viewer.runtime = nil

--- A table specifying the current safe area insets of the viewer. Use these values to avoid rendering important visible or interactive content under system interface areas.
---@type viewer.private.safeArea
viewer.safeArea = nil

--- Controls whether the viewer preserves a uniform resizing behavior when the view changes size. This is only supported on platforms where the viewer is resizable.
---@type boolean
viewer.uniformResizing = nil

--- Resizes the viewer to the specified width and height. This is only supported on platforms where the viewer is resizable.
---@param width number The new viewer width.
---@param height number The new viewer height.
function viewer.resize(width, height) end

--- A boolean value that indicates whether the viewer is paused.
---@type boolean
viewer.paused = nil

--- Controls whether runtime statistics are displayed in the viewer.
---@type boolean
viewer.displayStats = nil

--- Controls whether the viewer draws only when a redraw is requested.
---@type boolean
viewer.drawOnRequest = nil

--- Determines whether warnings should be displayed in the viewer. For example, warnings will be printed when using deprecated Codea APIs.
---@type boolean
viewer.showWarnings = nil

--- Closes the viewer and returns to the editor. Calling `viewer.close()` is functionally the same as pressing the on-screen Back button.
function viewer.close() end

--- Restarts the viewer, starting your project again. Calling `viewer.restart()` is functionally the same as pressing the on-screen Restart button.
function viewer.restart() end

--- Captures the rendered contents of the viewer and returns them as an `image`. This captures the rendered scene and does not include the sidebar UI.
---@return image
function viewer.snapshot() end

--- Shows a system alert. The `message` parameter specifies the message to display. The optional `title` parameter provides the title of the alert. If no title is specified, `"Alert"` is used.
---@param message string Message to display.
---@param title? string Alert title.
function viewer.alert(message, title) end

--- Shows a system share view for an image, string, or table of shareable items. This allows you to share content to a third-party service, save it to your device, or copy it to the pasteboard.
---@param data image|string|table Content to share.
function viewer.share(data) end

--- Returns whether the viewer is presenting an alert, share sheet, or another view that obscures the viewer.
---@type boolean
viewer.isPresenting = nil

--- Standard display mode. The output and parameters panes are visible, and the Back, Pause, Play and Reset buttons are shown.
---@type const
STANDARD = nil

--- Fullscreen display mode. An exit fullscreen button remains visible.
---@type const
FULLSCREEN = nil

--- Fullscreen display mode with all buttons hidden. Use `viewer.close()` if your project needs an explicit way to leave the viewer.
---@type const
FULLSCREEN_NO_BUTTONS = nil

--- Legacy runtime identifier.
---@type const
LEGACY = nil

--- Modern runtime identifier.
---@type const
MODERN = nil

voxel = {}

---@class voxel.world
---@overload fun(...): voxel.world
voxel.world = {}

--- The size of the world in blocks
---@type vec3
voxel.world.size = nil

--- The size of each chunk in blocks
---@type vec3
voxel.world.chunkSize = nil

--- The block types that can be used in this world
---@type voxel.blocks
voxel.world.blocks = nil

--- Gets the block at a coordinate
---@param coord vec3 The coordinate of the block (or x, y, z)
---@return voxel.block
---@return color
function voxel.world:get(coord) end

--- Sets the block at a coordinate. Coordinates outside of the world are ignored
---@param coord vec3 The coordinate of the block (or x, y, z)
---@param block string The block name, block type or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to multiply the block’s texture by
function voxel.world:set(coord, block, tint) end

--- Fills every block between two corners, including the corners themselves
---@param min vec3 One corner of the box (or x, y, z)
---@param max vec3 The opposite corner of the box (or x, y, z)
---@param block string The block name, block type or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to multiply the block’s texture by
function voxel.world:box(min, max, block, tint) end

--- Fills every block within `radius` blocks of `center`
---@param center vec3 The center of the sphere (or x, y, z)
---@param radius number The radius of the sphere in blocks
---@param block string The block name, block type or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to multiply the block’s texture by
function voxel.world:sphere(center, radius, block, tint) end

--- Fills a line of blocks from `start` to `end`, including both ends
---@param start vec3 The first block of the line (or x, y, z)
---@param p2 vec3 The last block of the line (or x, y, z)
---@param block string The block name, block type or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to multiply the block’s texture by
function voxel.world:line(start, p2, block, tint) end

--- How far (in blocks) from the `voxel.world.focus` chunks are generated and drawn when the world has a generator. Chunks a little further away than this are unloaded. Defaults to `128`
---@type number
voxel.world.viewDistance = nil

--- The point (in scene space) that chunks are generated around, such as the position of a player. When `nil` (the default) the scene’s camera is used
---@type vec3
voxel.world.focus = nil

--- `true` while chunks within the view distance are still being generated or built, useful for showing a loading screen
---@type boolean
voxel.world.loading = nil

--- The material blocks are drawn with, the voxel material unless it’s changed. Cutout and translucent blocks are drawn with copies of it, which follow any changes made to it. Set it to `nil` to go back to a new voxel material
---@type material
voxel.world.material = nil

--- Fills the world using a generator function as chunks come within `voxel.world.viewDistance` of the `voxel.world.focus`. Chunks that move out of range are unloaded, unless they have been changed since they were generated. Calling `generate` again replaces the generator and starts generating from scratch
--- 
--- The function is called with a `voxel.chunk` to fill, on background threads so the game keeps running smoothly while the world is generated. Each thread runs the code from `tab` in its own Lua environment, so the function can use the code in that tab, the standard `math`, `string` and `table` libraries, `noise`, `hash`, `color` and `print`, but not globals or functions from your other tabs. Anything else it needs is passed in through `params`, which is copied and available to the generator as the global `params`
--- 
--- `math.random` is seeded for each chunk from its position (and `params.seed` when it is set), so a world generates the same way every time
--- 
--- `hash(x[, y[, z]])` gives a number from `0` up to `1` for a position, which is always the same for that position (and `params.seed`), whichever chunk asks for it. Neighbouring chunks can use it to agree on things that cross the edges between them, where `math.random` would give each chunk different numbers. Coordinates are rounded down to whole numbers, and neighbouring positions give unrelated numbers (use `noise` for numbers that change smoothly). Give it an extra coordinate for more than one number at a position
--- 
--- Blocks can only be changed in chunks that have been generated, changes elsewhere are ignored
---@param tab asset The project tab containing the generator, such as asset.Generation
---@param functionName string The name of the function in that tab to call for each chunk
---@param params? table Values for the generator. Only numbers, strings, booleans and tables of these can be passed
function voxel.world:generate(tab, functionName, params) end

--- Removes every block from the world, and stops generating
function voxel.world:clear() end

--- Copies a `voxel.model` into the world as blocks of one type, each tinted with its voxel’s color. The model’s voxel `(0, 0, 0)` goes at `origin`, and its empty voxels leave the world as it is. Placing with a `nil` block removes the blocks the model covers instead
---@param model voxel.model The model to place
---@param origin vec3 Where the model’s first voxel goes (or x, y, z)
---@param block string The block name or voxel.block to build the model from, or nil to remove blocks
function voxel.world:place(model, origin, block) end

--- Saves the world’s blocks to a `.cvox` file
--- 
--- A generated world only saves the chunks that have been changed since they were generated, as its generator can make the rest again. This keeps saves small, even for very large worlds
---@param asset asset Where to save the world, ending in .cvox
function voxel.world:save(asset) end

--- Replaces the world’s blocks with those in a file saved by `voxel.world.save()`
--- 
--- Blocks are matched to the world’s block types by name. A block type the world doesn’t have yet is added without a texture, and can be given one afterwards with `voxel.blocks.add()`. The world must have the same chunk size as the one that was saved. Chunks beyond the world’s size are left out
--- 
--- To load a generated world, call `voxel.world.generate()` first and then `load`. Chunks in the file replace the generated ones, and the generator goes on making the rest
---@param asset asset The .cvox file to load
function voxel.world:load(asset) end

--- Finds the first block along a ray. The ray is in scene space (the same space as [`camera.screenToRay()`](camera.json#camera.screenToRay)), so it works however the world’s entity is moved, rotated or scaled
---@param origin vec3 The start of the ray
---@param direction vec3 The direction of the ray
---@param distance? number The maximum distance to search
---@return voxel.hit
function voxel.world:raycast(origin, direction, distance) end

