--- Fills every block between two corners, including the corners themselves
---@param x1 any 
---@param y1 any 
---@param z1 any 
---@param x2 any 
---@param y2 any 
---@param z2 any 
---@param block string The block name or id, or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to tint the blocks with
function voxel.chunk:box(x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2, block, tint) end

--- Fills every block within `radius` blocks of a center block
---@param x any 
---@param y any 
---@param z any 
---@param radius any 
---@param block string The block name or id, or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to tint the blocks with
function voxel.chunk:sphere(x, y, z, radius, block, tint) end

--- Fills a line of blocks between two blocks, including the ends
---@param x1 any 
---@param y1 any 
---@param z1 any 
---@param x2 any 
---@param y2 any 
---@param z2 any 
---@param block string The block name or id, or nil for empty
---@param tint? color A color to tint the blocks with
function voxel.chunk:line(x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2, block, tint) end

---@class voxel.blocks
---@overload fun(...): voxel.blocks
voxel.blocks = {}

--- Adds a new block type, or changes the existing type with the same name. The definition table can contain:
--- 
--- - `texture` - an image asset used for every face, or a table of assets by face
--- - `color` - a tint for every face, or a table of colors by face
--- - `translucent` - `true` for blended blocks like water and glass. Faces between translucent blocks of the same type are hidden
--- - `cutout` - `true` for blocks with see-through holes like leaves, where pixels with less than half alpha are not drawn
--- - `roughness` and `metallic` - from `0` to `1`, used in place of the world material’s
--- - `emissive` - how brightly the block glows in its own color, from `0` to `8`, used in place of the world material’s
--- 
--- Faces are named `all`, `sides`, `top`, `bottom`, `north`, `south`, `east` and `west` (most specific wins). Blocks without a texture are plain white, tinted by their color
--- 
--- Any other values in the definition become custom properties of the block type
---@param name string The name of the block type
---@param definition? table How the block looks, along with any custom properties
---@return voxel.block
function voxel.blocks:add(name, definition) end

--- Gets a block type by name or id
---@param name string The name or id of the block type
---@return voxel.block
function voxel.blocks:get(name) end

--- Gets every block type, in the order they were added
---@return table
function voxel.blocks:all() end

--- Removes every block type, including the standard blocks. Blocks already in the world using a removed type are no longer drawn
function voxel.blocks:clear() end

---@class voxel.block
---@overload fun(...): voxel.block
voxel.block = {}

--- The name of this block type
---@type string
voxel.block.name = nil

--- A number identifying this block type within its world
---@type integer
voxel.block.id = nil

--- Whether this block type is blended, like water and glass
---@type boolean
voxel.block.translucent = nil

--- Whether this block type has see-through holes, like leaves
---@type boolean
voxel.block.cutout = nil

--- The roughness of this block type, from `0` to `1`, or `nil` to use the world material’s
---@type number
voxel.block.roughness = nil

--- How metallic this block type is, from `0` to `1`, or `nil` to use the world material’s
---@type number
voxel.block.metallic = nil

--- How brightly this block type glows in its own color, from `0` to `8`, or `nil` to use the world material’s
---@type number
voxel.block.emissive = nil

---@class voxel.model
---@overload fun(...): voxel.model
voxel.model = {}

--- The size of the model in voxels
---@type vec3
voxel.model.size = nil

--- A mesh that draws the model, with one unit per voxel and its origin at the corner of voxel `(0, 0, 0)`. Faces between neighbouring voxels are left out and corners are shaded by ambient occlusion, like a `voxel.world`. It’s drawn with a new voxel material, or any other material given to its entity
--- 
--- The same mesh is returned until the model changes, so it can be shared by many entities. Changing a voxel makes a new mesh the next time this is read, entities already using the old mesh keep showing it
---@type mesh
voxel.model.mesh = nil

--- Gets the color of a voxel
---@param coord vec3 The coordinate of the voxel (or x, y, z)
---@return color
function voxel.model:get(coord) end

--- Sets the color of a voxel, or empties it. Coordinates outside of the model are ignored
---@param coord vec3 The coordinate of the voxel (or x, y, z)
---@param color color The voxel’s color, or nil for empty
function voxel.model:set(coord, color) end

--- Makes a copy of the model. Changing either one leaves the other as it is, which makes copies useful for undo in an editor. The copy shares the model’s `voxel.model.mesh` until one of them changes, so a copy made after the mesh is read can be shown again without being meshed
---@return voxel.model
function voxel.model:copy() end

--- Replaces the model’s size and voxels with another model’s, leaving the other model as it is. Entities keep showing the old mesh until they’re given `voxel.model.mesh` again
---@param other voxel.model The model to copy from
function voxel.model:assign(other) end

--- Saves the model to a file. The file’s extension picks the format: `.cvox` for the Voxel Editor, or `.vox` for MagicaVoxel. MagicaVoxel files can hold at most 255 different colors and 256 voxels in each dimension, larger or more colorful models can be saved as `.cvox`
---@param asset asset Where to save the model, ending in .cvox or .vox
function voxel.model:save(asset) end
